e interests of the Chinese  Communist Party so how effective will  this task force really be that the  government has appointed I think it'll  be effective in getting the universities  themselves to do more because ultimately  the real burden should be on our  universities to do more to integrate  Chinese students to protect them from  surveillance and intimidation by the  Chinese Communist Party and it's only by  getting universities on board that I  think the government can deal with these  issues the alternate argument of course  here would be that Chinese students or  if you call them pro-beijing students  they have an equal right to protest and  to voice their opinions on Australian  universities and that should not be seen  as interference would that be fair to  say I think I think that is very fair I  think in Australia as a free and  democratic society we do tolerate voices  from all kinds of perspectives but the  difference and part of the concern here  is that the Chinese government does seem  to be having a role in some of these  activities so rather than being genuine  expressions of political or patriotic  sentiments these are activities  facilitated by the Chinese government  and also through also facilitated by the  propaganda bubble that it is Corin  overseas through platform  like the social media app WeChat Alex  Yosuke from the Australian strategic  policy institute thank you very much for  that thank you
 

